WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:02.760
SANDEEP DINESH: As you start
to build more and more services

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.710
on top of Kubernetes,
simple tasks

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.760
start to get more complicated.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.810
For example, teams can't
create services or deployments

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.380
with the same name.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:13.170
If you have thousands
of pod, just

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.220
listing them all
would take some time,

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.280
let alone actually
administering them.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.850
And these are just the
tip of the iceberg.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.180
In this episode of
"Kubernetes Best Practices,"

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.600
let's take a look at how
namespaces can make managing

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.100
Kubernetes resources easier.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.690
So what exactly is a namespace?

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.240
You can think of a namespace
as a virtual cluster

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.300
inside your Kubernetes cluster.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.130
You can have multiple namespaces
inside a single Kubernetes

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.000
cluster.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.950
And they're all isolated
from each other.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:51.120
They can help you and your teams
with organization, security,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.880
and even performance.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.710
In most Kubernetes
distributions,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.560
the cluster comes out of the
box with the namespace called,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.460
default. In fact, there are
actually three namespaces that

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:08.220
Kubernetes ships with, default,
kube system, and kube public.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.130
Kube public really isn't
used for much right now.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.470
And it's usually a good idea
to leave kube system alone,

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:18.390
especially in a managed system
like Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.250
This leaves the
default namespace

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.640
as the place where your
services and apps are created.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.880
There's absolutely nothing
special about this namespace,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.320
except that the Kubernetes
tooling is set up

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.440
out of the box to
use this namespace

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.330
and you can't delete it.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.950
While it's great
for getting started

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.900
and smaller
production systems, I

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.770
would recommend against using
it in large production systems.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.230
This is because it's
very easy for a team

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.140
to accidentally overwrite,
or disrupt another team

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.420
without even realizing it.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.360
Instead, you should
create multiple namespaces

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.340
and use them to
segment your services

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.970
into manageable chunks.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:59.240
Creating a namespace can be
done with a single command.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.910
If you want to create a
namespace called test,

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:05.810
you would run kubectl
create namespace test.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.570
Or you could create a YAML
file and apply it just

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:11.490
like any other
Kubernetes resource.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.550
You can see all the namespaces
with the following command,

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.130
kubectl get namespace.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.690
You can see the three built-in
namespaces spaces, as well as

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.480
the new namespace called test.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.040
Let's take a look at a
simple YAML to create a pod.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.550
You may notice that there
is no mention of a namespace

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:29.880
anywhere.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.250
If you kubectl
apply on this file,

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.980
it'll create a pod in the
current active namespace.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.120
This will be the default
namespace unless you change it.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.280
Now, there's two ways
to explicitly tell

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.000
Kubernetes which namespace you
want your resource created in.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.490
The first way, is using
the namespace flag

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.540
when creating the resource.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.580
The second way is to specify
a namespace in the YAML

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.400
declaration.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.030
Now, if you specify a
namespace in the YAML,

00:02:57.030 --> 00:03:00.510
the resource will always be
created in that namespace.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.040
If you try to use
another namespace

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.736
by using the namespace
flag, the command will fail.

00:03:05.736 --> 00:03:07.110
Now, if you try
to find your pod,

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.450
you may have noticed
you can't find it.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.340
This is because all
commands are run

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.770
against a current
active namespace.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:17.055
To find your pod, you need
to use the namespace flag.

00:03:17.055 --> 00:03:18.974
Now, this can get
annoying quickly,

00:03:18.974 --> 00:03:20.640
especially if you're
a developer working

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.580
on a team that uses its own
namespace for everything

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:25.530
and don't want to use
the namespace flag

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.090
for every single command.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.400
Let's see how we can fix that.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.620
Out of the box, your
active namespace

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.570
is the default namespace.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.610
Like we saw before,
unless you specify

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.650
a namespace in the
resource's YAML,

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.610
all Kubernetes commands will
use the active namespace.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:44.280
Now unfortunately, trying to
manage an active namespace

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.520
with kubectl can be a pain.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.680
Fortunately, there's a
really good tool called

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.530
Kubens that makes it a breeze.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.600
When you run the
kubens command, you

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.510
should see all the namespaces
with the active namespace

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.010
highlighted.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.860
To switch your active
namespace to the test namespace

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.290
you can just run kubens test.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.770
Now you can see the test
namespace is active.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.080
If you run kubectl
commands, the namespace

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.570
will now be test
instead of default.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.700
This means you don't need
the namespace flag to see

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.540
the pod in the test namespace.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.870
So namespaces are
hidden from each other,

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:21.459
but they're not isolated.

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:23.620
A service in one
namespace can talk

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.380
to a service in another
namespace pretty easily.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.340
This can often be very useful.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.760
For example, having your
service for your team

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.230
talk to another namespace
with another team's service.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.230
Normally, when your app wants
to access a Kubernetes service,

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.050
you can just use the built-in
DNS service discovery

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:43.830
and just point your app
at the services name.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:45.730
However, you can
create a service

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:49.150
for the same name in
multiple namespaces.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.890
Thankfully, it's easy
to get around this

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:54.670
by using the expanded
form of the DNS address.

00:04:54.670 --> 00:04:56.800
Services in Kubernetes
expose their endpoint point

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.690
using a common DNS pattern.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.030
It looks like this.

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:03.460
Normally, you just
need the service's name

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:07.370
and DNS will automatically
resolve to the full address.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:11.020
However, if you need to access
a service in another namespace,

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.750
just use the service name
plus the namespace name.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.110
For example, if you want
to connect to the database

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.300
service in the
test namespace, you

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.501
can use the following
address database.test.

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:24.250
And if you want to
connect to the database

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.350
service in the
prod namespace, you

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:30.120
can use the following
address database.prod.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:33.660
Of course, sometimes you do
want to isolate and limit access

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.160
to a namespace.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.770
Kubernetes lets you do
this with network policies.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.550
Stay tuned for a
future episode, where

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.480
we'll deep dive into these.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:45.280
A common question I get is,
how many namespaces to create?

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:46.740
And for what purpose?

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:49.270
What exactly is a
manageable chunk?

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.270
If you make too many namespaces,
they'll just get in your way.

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.710
But if you make too few,
you'll lose the benefits.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:58.860
I think there are four major
stages that each company goes

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.370
through and they have their
own organizational structure.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.100
Depending on the stage that
your project or company is in,

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.040
you can adopt the relevant
namespace strategy.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.765
So imagine you're part of a
small team that's working on

00:06:11.765 --> 00:06:15.120
maybe 5 to 10 microservices and
you can easily bring everyone

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.890
into the same room.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:18.750
In this situation,
it makes sense

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:21.060
to launch all production
services into the default

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:22.650
namespace.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.840
You might want to have a
production and development

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.640
namespace, if you
want to get fancy.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.890
But you know, you're probably
testing your development

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:30.990
environment on your local
machine using something

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.250
like Minikube.

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:33.760
Now, the scenario changes.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.520
You have a rapidly
growing team that's

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.782
working a 10 or
more microservices.

00:06:37.782 --> 00:06:39.240
It's necessary at
this point to use

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.460
multiple clusters,
or namespaces,

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.950
for production and development.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.380
You may start to split the
team into multiple sub-teams

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:48.384
that each have their
own microservices.

00:06:48.384 --> 00:06:49.800
And then each of
these teams might

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.252
choose their own namespace
for easier manageability.

00:06:53.252 --> 00:06:55.945
While everyone might know how
the complete system works,

00:06:55.945 --> 00:06:58.320
it's getting harder and harder
to coordinate every change

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.760
with everyone else.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.100
Trying to spin up the full
stack on your local machine

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.750
is just getting more
complicated every day.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:08.610
Now, at large companies everyone
doesn't know everyone else.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.190
Teams are working on features
that other teams might not even

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:12.570
know about.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:15.480
Now, teams are communicating
using service contracts

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.790
and maybe they're using
service meshes like Istio

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:19.560
to coordinate communication.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.130
Trying to run the whole stack
locally is just impossible.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:26.400
Using a Kubernetes aware
CD system like Spinnaker

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.280
is highly recommended.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:30.990
And at this point,
each team definitely

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:33.150
needs their own namespace.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:35.630
Each team might even opt
for multiple namespaces

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:38.340
to run their development
and production environments.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.070
Setting up [? R-back ?] and
resource quotas is a good idea

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.000
as well.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.540
And we'll cover those
in future episodes.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.770
Multiple clusters start to
make sense at this point,

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.240
but they really might
not be necessary.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:52.405
And finally, there are
large enterprise companies.

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:54.780
There are groups that don't
even know about the existence

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.190
of other groups.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.880
Groups might as well be external
companies and services are

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.950
consumed through
well-documented APIs.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.870
Each group has
multiple teams and have

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:05.490
multiple microservices.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:08.610
Using all the tools I previously
mentioned are necessary.

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:10.920
And people should not be
deploying services by hand.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.750
And in fact, they
should be locked out

00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:15.007
of namespaces they don't own.

00:08:15.007 --> 00:08:16.590
At this point, it
probably makes sense

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.780
to have multiple clusters
to reduce the blast

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:20.910
radius of poorly
configured applications

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.730
and to make billing and
resource management easier.

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.500
As the number of
microservices and teams

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.260
using Kubernetes in your
organization increases,

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:30.270
namespaces can really
make Kubernetes

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:33.330
a lot more manageable and
give you increased control,

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.909
security, and flexibility.

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:40.250
I'll see you on the next episode
of "Kubernetes Best Practices."

